cartridge "FLAGS/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1990"

' FLAGS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Flags", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk, January
' 1990 (Commodore 64, BASIC; no author named in the listing, which
' carries only the COMPUTE! Publications copyright). Made for Amy Studio
' by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (orig/flags.bas): 41 flags of the world.
'  LEARN FLAGS: page through the flags with their names.
'  ONE PLAYER: 10 flags. Each shows with three names (the right one and two
'   others at random); a timer counts down from 15. Choose a name with the
'   stick and press the button: right adds timer x 50, wrong takes off
'   timer x 30 (never below 0), out of time scores nothing. The flags come
'   without repeats until all 41 have been asked.
'  TWO PLAYERS: both choose at once, each with a joystick; the first to
'   press the button answers for that flag. First past 5000 ends the game.
'
' From the listing: the 41 flags and names (DATA 1530-1930), three choices
' shuffled, the 15 count, the scores (lines 760-850), 10 flags (RR > 10)
' for one player, over 5000 for two, no repeat until all used (AA()).
'
' Liberties: the flags are drawn as pictures from tiles (art.py) with their
' real emblems where the C64 used a sprite or nothing; colours fixed where
' the listing is clearly wrong (LIBYA plain green as in 1990 instead of
' red/white/black, BELGIUM black/yellow/red instead of reversed, SIERRA
' LEONE blue instead of red, OMAN white instead of yellow, BANGLADESH green
' and red, orange for INDIA/NIGER/IVORY COAST, CAMEROON/SENEGAL star
' colours un-swapped); "NEW GUINEA" renamed GUINEA (the flag drawn is
' Guinea's; Papua New Guinea's is different). Other names kept as in 1990.
' The timer ticks once a second (the BASIC loop ran roughly that fast).
' Two players see one list with an arrow each (P1 left, P2 right) instead
' of two lists. The end names the winner. Joystick menu instead of keys
' 1/2/3; learn mode with left/right and the button instead of + - Q.
'
' Controls: up/down choose, a side button answers. Learn mode: left/right
' change flag, a button goes back to the title.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29; tiles from code 128; screens drawn
' with the NMI off; music first in the sound table; the title tune plays
' once; nothing flashes.

